const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens bathe?', a: 'Yes. Ravens bathe frequently in water — standing in shallow water and dipping their heads and bodies, then ruffling and shaking their feathers. They also bathe in rain (rain-bathing, spreading wings on a perch during rainfall) and roll in snow (snow-bathing). Bathing is a regular, daily or near-daily behaviour in birds with access to water, combined with preening to maintain feather condition.' },
  { q: 'What is allopreening in ravens?', a: 'Allopreening is mutual preening — one bird preening the head and neck of another. In ravens, allopreening is a social bonding behaviour primarily observed between pair-bonded mates, but also between closely bonded non-mated individuals. The head and neck are regions a bird cannot effectively self-preen; allopreening from a trusted partner keeps these feathers in good condition and reinforces pair bonds. Allopreening occurs year-round in bonded raven pairs.' },
